---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SolarCalculator from '../../../components/SolarCalculator.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Solar Power System Calculator"
  description="Size a complete off-grid or backup solar system — inverter, battery bank, panels, charge controller, cables, breakers and surge protection — then run a full techno-economic analysis with NPV, payback, IRR, LCOE and ROI. Runs entirely in your browser."
>
  <article class="tool-page wrap">
    <h1>Solar Power System Calculator</h1>
    <p class="tool-intro">
      Build a load list, get a complete bill of materials, then find out
      whether the system actually pays for itself. The sizing chain runs load
      audit → inverter → battery bank → PV array → charge controller → cables
      → breakers → surge protection, and the economics layer works through
      capital cost, LCOE, NPV, benefit-cost ratio, payback, IRR and return on
      investment. Everything runs in your browser — no account, no upload.
    </p>

    <SolarCalculator />

    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        <strong>Load class changes the arithmetic, not just the label.</strong>
        Resistive loads (heaters, filament lighting) and capacitive loads
        (electronics with switch-mode supplies) count at their running power.
        Inductive loads — motors, compressors, pumps — count at running power
        multiplied by their start-up surge factor, because they briefly draw
        several times their rated power when they switch on. The tool suggests
        a class from the appliance name; confirm or correct it, because
        misclassifying a motor is the fastest way to undersize an inverter.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Costs are placeholders until you replace them.</strong> The
        shipped component prices and exchange rates are order-of-magnitude
        starting points for the Nigerian market, carrying the date they were
        last reviewed. They are not quotes, and solar pricing moves fast. A
        payback period or NPV is only ever as good as the prices behind it, so
        put your own supplier figures in before relying on any of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool sizes equipment and models economics. It is not a substitute
        for a design signed off by a qualified electrical engineer, and
        nothing here authorises anyone to carry out electrical installation
        work. DC arc faults and battery short circuits are dangerous.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How the calculation works</h2>

    <h3>Sizing</h3>
    <p>
      The load audit splits your appliances by class and totals them — R and C
      at running power, I at running power times surge — then applies a safety
      factor to give the design load. The inverter is sized to the largest of
      three competing demands: the continuous load with its future buffer and
      efficiency allowance, the continuous load plus the largest single motor
      surge, and the class-based design total. The battery bank comes from
      daily energy, the fraction drawn from storage, days of autonomy, loss
      factor and depth of discharge, then rounds up to whole series strings at
      the system voltage. The array is sized from daily energy, a factor of
      safety, the performance ratio and your site's peak sun hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cables are sized twice — once for voltage drop and once for
      current-carrying capacity — and the larger answer wins. That matters:
      a cable can pass a 3% voltage-drop check at a cross-section that would
      overheat under the same current, so checking only one of the two is how
      DIY solar installations catch fire. Breakers are then sized to protect
      the cable rather than the load, and capped so they can never exceed the
      cable's ampacity.
    </p>

    <h3>Techno-economics</h3>
    <p>
      The economic model follows equations (1) to (13) of Sobamowo and Ojolo's
      2018 techno-economic analysis of biomass gasification in Nigeria,
      adapted for solar, with a fourteenth equation added for return on
      investment. Three things genuinely differ from the biomass case. There
      is no fuel, so the fuel-cost term is zero — which is most of why a solar
      system can clear a benefit-cost ratio above 1 at a far lower tariff than
      a gasification plant. Storage has to be replaced part-way through the
      system life, so a battery replacement term is added to the O&M equation.
      And panel output degrades, so the levelised cost of electricity is
      indexed year by year instead of using a constant-output shortcut.
    </p>
    <p>
      Return on investment is reported three ways on purpose: simple lifetime
      ROI as the headline because it is the number people recognise, the
      discounted figure immediately beneath it, and a simple annual rate. A
      system can show a healthy simple ROI and a negative discounted ROI once
      a realistic cost of capital is applied. When that happens the tool says
      so rather than showing you the flattering number alone.
    </p>

    <p class="tool-footnote">
      Sizing figures follow standard off-grid design practice; the economic
      model is adapted from G. M. Sobamowo and S. J. Ojolo, "Techno-Economic
      Analysis of Biomass Energy Utilization through Gasification Technology
      for Sustainable Energy Production and Economic Development in Nigeria,"
      <em>Journal of Energy</em>, vol. 2018, Article ID 4860252. See our
      <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> for what this tool is (and isn't) a
      substitute for.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
